  "value_sources": {
    "decided": "2026-11-12, after the Phase 2 audit (docs/PHASE2_AUDIT_2026-11-12.md); used only where the drawings give nothing better",
    "lighting_w_m2": "NCC 2022 Volume One Table J7D3a, maximum illumination power density for the space type (the most a compliant new fit-out may install): office (200 lx or more) 4.5, retail space 14, restaurant/café/bar 14, kitchen and food preparation 4, storage 1.5. Residential 5: J7D3(1), a sole-occupancy unit. Generic 6: Table J7D3a Note 1, an unlisted space lit for ordinary tasks (more than 320 and not more than 400 lx).",
    "occupancy_density_people_m2": "AS 1668.2-2002 Appendix A Table A1 (informative; AIRAH-hosted extract), net floor area per occupant where occupants aren't nominated: sales floors, basement and street 3.5 m² (0.286), offices 10 m² (0.1), dining rooms 1.5 m² (0.667), food preparation, serving and storage 3.5 m² (0.286), storage areas 10 m² (0.1). The standard calls these approximate and conservative. Generic 0.08 is uncited.",
    "outside_air": "7.5 L/s per person plus 0.3 L/s per m², unchanged: an office at 10 m² per person gets 10.5 L/s per person. AS 1668.2-2002 Table A1 lists 7.5 as the Grade 2 dilution index for offices and dining; its Grade 1 points to Tables 4.3 and 4.4, which the extract doesn't include. Secondary sources give 10 L/s per person for offices and dining in the 2012 and 2024 editions; not verified against the standard.",
    "people_activity": "AIRAH Technical Handbook 2007 p. 47, unchanged. ASHRAE Fundamentals Table 1 gives about 80/80 W for restaurant diners (secondary copies only, not verified)."
  },
